WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Shared memory can be fast because multiple processes access the same bytes.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.400
That speed changes the security and correctness model.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:15.100
Instead of receiving a completed message, a process may observe data while another process is writing it.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:21.280
Permissions, layout, synchronization, and lifecycle all become part of the contract.

5
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:28.600
A shared memory region should define ownership, size, version, initialization state, and valid fields.

6
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:34.780
If multiple program versions may interact, versioning and compatibility rules matter.

7
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:42.160
If the region contains offsets, counters, lengths, or arrays, those fields still need validation before use.

8
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:46.900
Shared memory does not make input trusted simply because it is local.

9
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:52.940
Readers and writers need synchronization to avoid inconsistent or partially updated state.

10
00:00:53.040 --> 00:01:05.680
The exact mechanism depends on the platform and design, but the principle is stable: a reader should know when data is complete enough to use, and a writer should not leave shared state in an ambiguous condition without a recovery rule.

11
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.840
Shared memory should not be used as a shortcut around authorization or message validation.

12
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:15.100
A process that can write shared state can influence another process.

13
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.960
That influence should be intentional and constrained.

14
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:24.500
Permissions on the region, peer identity where relevant, and internal consistency checks all matter.

15
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.740
Cleanup should handle stale, abandoned, or partially initialized regions safely.

16
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.680
A restart may find a leftover region from a previous instance.

17
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:39.460
The program should distinguish usable state from state that must be reinitialized or removed.

18
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.640
Without those rules, shared memory can turn recovery into guesswork.
